{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgiCqvR4zG3ff7CVzam1M","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HMS5GysCLAR65751ZbC24V","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"$WSROOT/laya/s1/opus-high-code-r2/step2/NOTES.md","content":"# Step 2 notes: card PIN -> cards\n\n## Change\n`router.py` adds one rule after the step-1 rules:\nif Laya says `account_security` and the text contains the word `PIN`\n(`\\bpins?\\b`, or \"personal identification number\"), return `cards`.\n\n## Why this shape\n- All 16 errors in `evidence.csv` are PIN messages that Laya sent to\n  `account_security`. No PIN message went to another department.\n- The rule fires only on `account_security`, so a PIN message that Laya\n  sends elsewhere (for example a fee to change a PIN, in `fees_fx`) keeps\n  its answer.\n- The word boundary keeps \"pinned\", \"spin\" and similar words out.\n- \"passcode\" (app login) does not match, so it stays in `account_security`.\n- Step-1 rules fire only on `disputes` or `cards`, so the two corrections\n  do not overlap. Step-1 code is unchanged.\n\n## Results\nCommand: `python -I check.py router.py evidence.csv` (venv Python).\n- Patched: evidence 32/32; 8/8 extra cases (step-1 rate cases, passcode,\n  \"pinned\", identity, PIN in `fees_fx`).\n- Original router: evidence 16/32 (negative test, so the check catches the bug).\n\n## Risk\nA message that says \"PIN\" but is really about the app login would move to\n`cards`. The evidence has no such case.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":552,"cache_read_input_tokens":65298,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":552},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f44c3227-6a4e-4b9b-8bb7-f8e7be09b636","uuid":"66354335-b5c5-4cea-9e8e-17025c3f0410","timestamp":"2026-11-04T16:29:40.005Z","request_id":"req_011CgiCqv9wPZ9oz3mR4gZEG","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01HMS5GysCLAR65751ZbC24V":{"file_path":"$WSROOT/laya/s1/opus-high-code-r2/step2/NOTES.md","content":"# Step 2 notes: card PIN -> cards\n\n## Change\n`router.py` adds one rule after the step-1 rules:\nif Laya says `account_security` and the text contains the word `PIN`\n(`\\bpins?\\b`, or \"personal identification number\"), return `cards`.\n\n## Why this shape\n- All 16 errors in `evidence.csv` are PIN messages that Laya sent to\n  `account_security`. No PIN message went to another department.\n- The rule fires only on `account_security`, so a PIN message that Laya\n  sends elsewhere (for example a fee to change a PIN, in `fees_fx`) keeps\n  its answer.\n- The word boundary keeps \"pinned\", \"spin\" and similar words out.\n- \"passcode\" (app login) does not match, so it stays in `account_security`.\n- Step-1 rules fire only on `disputes` or `cards`, so the two corrections\n  do not overlap. Step-1 code is unchanged.\n\n## Results\nCommand: `python -I check.py router.py evidence.csv` (venv Python).\n- Patched: evidence 32/32; 8/8 extra cases (step-1 rate cases, passcode,\n  \"pinned\", identity, PIN in `fees_fx`).\n- Original router: evidence 16/32 (negative test, so the check catches the bug).\n\n## Risk\nA message that says \"PIN\" but is really about the app login would move to\n`cards`. The evidence has no such case.\n"}}}
